## The RED is the weak shape, and saying so is the point

`npx vitest run --project worker test/task-groups.test.ts` before any production
change fails with **`Cannot find module '../src/shared/task-groups'`** — one
failed suite, zero executed cases. That is a module-resolution RED, not the
assertion RED phase 1 produced, and the two are not equally strong: a
module-not-found proves only that the file is absent. It cannot distinguish a
suite whose assertions are discriminative from one whose assertions are
nonsense, and it would look identical if the import path were simply mistyped.

It is recorded rather than dressed up because it is also the only honest RED
available here: the phase's whole deliverable is the module, and a test-first
suite for a module that does not exist yet cannot import it. Writing a stub to
get an assertion RED would be the implementer's work leaking into the test
stage. The precedent is this project's own — `src/shared/format.ts`,
`src/shared/connectivity.ts` and `src/shared/toast.ts` were each authored
test-first as brand-new modules during `ui-design-pass`.

Two things carry the weight the RED cannot:

1. **The import path is verified, not assumed.** The suite imports
   `../src/shared/task-groups`, which resolves to `src/shared/task-groups.ts` —
   byte-identical to the path the APPROVED plan's Task 1 and its `## Files to
   Change` row name. A mistyped path would produce this same error, so it was
   checked directly rather than inferred from the error message.
2. **The real proof is the GREEN, and it must arrive with zero test edits.**
   After the implementer runs, all 16 cases must pass against a suite whose
   `git diff` is empty. The plan's Level 3 gate is `git diff --quiet -- test/`,
   which fails if any test file was touched at all — so "the tests were bent to
   fit the implementation" is machine-checkable here rather than a matter of
   trust.

The load-bearing property of `groupTasks` is not membership — it is that the
function **partitions and never sorts**. The frozen read contract makes ordering
the API's guarantee and forbids re-deriving it in a client, so an implementation
with perfect bucket membership that sorted inside a bucket would be wrong while
looking right.

## Revision 1 — the defect B8 caught, and why it mattered

The first DRAFT was rejected on **R-RED-LEGITIMATE**, correctly, and the reason
was not the RED's shape — it was an assertion no correct implementation could
ever satisfy. Two different properties had been collapsed into one fixture:

- *the order inside each bucket is preserved* — true of `scrambled`, which
  interleaves buckets at the top level (O, O, T, U, O, T, N, U, N) precisely so
  that a sort inside a bucket would show;
- *concatenating the buckets reproduces the input* — true only when the input
  already arrives in bucket order, which is what the API always returns and what
  `scrambled` deliberately is not.

Asserted against `scrambled`, the second is false for every correct
implementation: `[O-mid, O-oldest, O-newest, T-second, T-first, …]` can never
equal `[O-mid, O-oldest, T-second, U-far, O-newest, …]`. The two tests ran the
same call and demanded mutually exclusive outcomes, so the manifest's own promise
that "all 16 cases must pass" was false as drafted — the implementer would have
been stuck at a correct implementation.

The fix keeps both properties and gives each its own fixture: `scrambled` still
carries the arrival-order, no-loss and object-identity cases, and a new
`inApiOrder` fixture — overdue ascending, then today, then future ascending, then
undated, exactly what `urgencyBucket` produces — carries the identity case, now
stated as *"is the identity function on a list already in the API's urgency
order"*. The comment in that test names the trap so it is not re-introduced.

**Both properties were then hand-verified against Task 1's literal algorithm
before the module existed** — a single pass, status first, `deadline ??
scheduledDate` compared as strings — confirming that `scrambled` yields
`overdue = [O-mid, O-oldest, O-newest]` (so a sorting implementation fails) and
that concatenating `inApiOrder`'s buckets reproduces the input exactly. That
simulation is deliberate compensation for the weak RED below: it is the only way
to know, before any code exists, that the assertions are satisfiable AND
discriminative rather than merely unrunnable.

## What the suite is built to catch

The `scrambled` fixture is deliberately NOT in date order inside any
bucket: three overdue Tasks arrive mid / oldest / newest, two "today" Tasks
arrive second / first, two upcoming arrive far / near. A sort would rewrite
every one of those sequences. Three cases pin the module from different angles —
the per-bucket arrival order over `scrambled`, the identity property over
`inApiOrder` (the shape the route actually returns), and object identity
(`toBe`, not `toEqual`) so a `.map()`-based implementation that returns copies is
caught too, which matters because the screen keys rows by object.

The purity block pins the other half of the module's contract: the same input
yields different buckets for three different `today` values (so no clock is
read), the input array is not mutated, and an empty list yields five empty
buckets rather than a partially-built object.

